# twoboot-knobs.env — TWOBOOT (rmbp-ledger B526): what the two-boot leg changes about THE IMAGE LINE, and why.
#
# The leg's knob set is DERIVED, never restated: `scripts/image-line.env` (FOLDBUILD B517, the one file the bench's image
# is built under) MINUS every `drop` below, PLUS every `add` below. A knob joins or leaves the image by a commit to
# image-line.env and reaches this leg on its own; this file only names the exceptions, ONE line each, with the reason.
# `boot-leg.sh` (B501) has no reusable derivation of the line (it maps a hard-coded feature list that predates
# image-line.env), so this is the one list — read by `scripts/twoboot.sh` and nothing else.
#
# Shape (twoboot.sh refuses anything else, NO VERDICT rc 2):
#   drop UNAOS_<KNOB>            # why it is metal-only (QEMU cannot model it, or no compile reads it)
#   add  UNAOS_<KNOB>=<value>    # what the leg's fixture needs that the image line does not carry
# A `:?` (bench-local, [media]) knob on the line that is not dropped here is REFUSED by twoboot.sh: name it here first.
# Never a SELFBUILD/LLD knob in either column (twoboot.sh asserts it, as foldleg does).

# --- dropped: metal-only ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
drop UNAOS_WIFI_FW_PATH   # [media] a bench-local path arroyo stages onto the CARD's UnaFS /FIRMWARE/ (esp-x86 only); no compile reads it and QEMU has no BCM4331 to load it into

# --- added: the fixture ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
add UNAOS_LOGIN=1         # the login feature drives the first-user form (boot 1) and the login screen (boot 2); already on the line — asserted, not trusted
add UNAOS_USBSERIAL=1     # BUILDER knob, not a kernel feature: QEMU attaches the FTDI FT232 the metal wire is (target/ftdi.log), so the serial sink rides the USB pump exactly as SESSIONWEDGE's did
